3.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: In the scene as it is normally printed in English the overcoat / uniform coat that Ochumelov is wearing is brought back into the narrative each time his attitude to the dog, and therefore to “who might be watching”, changes. 

In the Garnett/Constance Garnett type text this is the four re-entries of the garment:

* the approach in the new / great overcoat with the astrakhan collar, the outward sign of his petty official self-importance;
* the moment he becomes deferential towards what he takes to be the General’s dog and the coat is noted as buttoned up / kept neat;
* the moment he swings to the opposite pole and declares the dog a stray and the coat is noted as unbuttoned / flung open in anger;
* the final re-adjustment of the coat when the crowd again forces him to hedge and he re-buttons / smooths himself again.

The three substantive descriptive shifts are usually quoted as three – the initial, the angry and the craven re-adjustment – which in the printed text work out as four explicit mentions/re-descriptions of the coat/overcoat. That is the chameleon effect: the garment is made to mirror Ochumelov’s fearful, status-driven mentality as it changes with every rumour about the dog’s owner.

4.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: In the Russian original the шинель / overcoat is a leit-motif that is picked up each time Ochumelov’s social colour changes. 

In substance Chekhov gives the coat three distinct descriptive readings, one for each marked change of the inspector’s attitude to the dog / to the crowd. In the usual Russian text the word шинель is picked up four times in the narrative, the three descriptive moments being :

* the first appearance, the inspector comes up ‘in his overcoat’, buttoned and official;
* when the dog is first taken to be a common street dog the overcoat is pulled tight/‘buttoned up’ round him, the gesture of fearful self-protection;
* after the dog is identified – first as the general’s, then as not the general’s – the coat is again opened / loosened / flung about, the outward sign of his sudden swagger and then of his collapse.

Those three descriptive uses are what the criticism normally refers to as the coat-symbol of Ochumelov’s chameleon-like fearfulness; in a straight lexical count the word шинель occurs four times in the 1884 text, the three passages above being the three meaningful descriptions of it.

