1.text:
The coat is the objective correlative of Ochumelov’s chameleon shifts and it is brought back into the scene every time his colour changes.

In the text as it is normally read in English, Garnett and the standard English prints, that is four explicit visual/evaluative returns of the garment:

* the entry, “in a new overcoat / greatcoat / pea-jacket with shiny buttons / astrakhan collar”, the establishing image of petty official self-importance;
* the anxious / self-protective fussing with / buttoning up of the overcoat when the crowd is hostile and he fears being soiled;
* the loosening / unbuttoning / flinging open of the coat when the dog looks like General Zhigalov’s and he becomes expansive and swaggering;
* the final re-buttoning / smoothing / brushing of the overcoat on exit when he collapses back into servile fear.

That is the initial description plus three re-descriptions that mark the three attitude shifts. 

That pattern is replicated in the 1884 Russian original. The lexeme шинель/шинели occurs five times in the scene, four of those occurrences with a descriptive/evaluative gloss, i.e. the same four-fold chameleon marking. In substance criticism normally talks about three meaningful descriptive shifts after the initial establishing appearance, which is why the figure is often quoted as three shifts / four mentions.

The four-fold marking is the canonical figure cited for the coat-symbol of Ochumelov’s fearful, status-driven mentality.

2.text:
In the printed scene the garment is used as a running objective correlative and it is brought back into the text each time Ochumelov/Otchumyelov changes colour.

In the Garnett-type English the coat/overcoat/greatcoat/pea-jacket is given an explicit visual/evaluative gloss four times:

* the establishing entrance, the new smart overcoat with shiny buttons / astrakhan collar, the sign of petty official self-importance;
* the self-conscious/buttoned-up moment when the crowd is hostile and he wants not to soil the new garment;
* the expansive/unbuttoned/flung-open moment when the dog is thought to be General Zhigalov’s and he swaggers;
* the final re-buttoning/smoothing/brushing on the way out when he collapses back into servile fearfulness.

That is the canonical four-fold re-entry that criticism quotes as the chameleon effect. It is three substantive attitude shifts, initial establishment + angry + craven re-adjustment, rendered in the English text as four explicit mentions/re-descriptions.

In the 1884 Russian the leit-motif is the same. The lexeme шинель/шинели is used five times in the narrative, four of them with a descriptive/evaluative gloss, i.e. the initial appearance in the new шинель and the three marked re-adjustments of it. In substance the three meaningful descriptive readings are the ones normally isolated as the coat-symbol of his fearful, status-driven mentality, which in a straight lexical count appear as four glossed pick-ups of the word шинель.

That is the four explicit descriptive/evaluative references that are normally cited, corresponding to the three meaningful shifts plus the initial establishment.

