1.text:
The coat is the running objective correlative for Ochumelov/Otchumyelov and it is picked up by the narrator with an explicit visual/evaluative gloss every time his social colour changes.

In the 1884 Russian original and in the Garnett-type English tradition that is reproduced in the standard prints the garment is brought back four times with a descriptive/evaluative gloss:

* the establishing entrance: Ochumelov comes up “в новой шинели”, “in a new overcoat / pea-jacket / greatcoat with shiny buttons / astrakhan collar”, the new smart official garment as the outward sign of petty official self-importance;
* the fearful self-protection: when the dog is first taken for a common street dog the шинель/overcoat is noted buttoned-up / pulled tight / fussed with, the gesture of anxiety and of not wanting to soil the new garment;
* the expansive / swaggering opening: when the dog is thought to be General Zhigalov’s the coat is loosened / unbuttoned / flung open as he becomes expansive and swaggering;
* the exit re-adjustment: on leaving the crowd he re-buttons / smooths / brushes the overcoat again, the final collapse back into servile fearfulness.

That is the canonical four-fold re-entry of the coat-symbol in the scene, an initial establishing description plus three re-descriptions that mark the three attitude shifts. Lexically шинель/шинели occurs five times in the 1884 text, four of them with the descriptive/evaluative gloss just above, which is normally quoted in criticism as three substantive chameleon shifts, i.e. four explicit descriptive mentions in all.

